1:
`week2/generated/day3-practice-unique.spec.ts`:

```ts
import { test, expect } from "@playwright/test";

test("a fresh first name on every run", async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto("https://demo.automationtesting.in/Register.html");

  const firstName = "Learner" + Math.random().toString(36).slice(2, 8);
  console.log("generated:", firstName);

  const field = page.getByPlaceholder("First Name");
  await field.fill(firstName);

  await expect(field).toHaveValue(firstName);
});
```

Run it twice and compare the two `generated:` lines — different every time.

**Why the value goes in a variable first.** The temptation is to inline the expression into `fill()`. Do that and the assertion has nothing to compare against: `Math.random()` gives a *different* string the second time you call it, so the test would assert the field holds a value it was never given. Generate once, hold it, use it in both places.

**Why unique data at all.** The course chose it over three obvious alternatives. Deleting the record afterwards leaves the suite broken whenever a run fails mid-way. A file of ten prepared names runs out on the eleventh run. A timestamp collides when two tests start in the same second. A random suffix makes the duplicate check simply unreachable.

> `Math.random().toString(36).slice(2, 8)` gives six alphanumeric characters. `Math.floor(Math.random() * 100000)` is equally fine where the field only accepts digits.

2:
```ts
import { test, expect } from "@playwright/test";

test("pick a real option at random and prove it exists before selecting it", async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto("https://demo.automationtesting.in/Register.html");

  // Read the options the page ACTUALLY has - never guess at them.
  const values = await page.locator("#Skills option").evaluateAll(
    (options) => options.map((o) => (o as HTMLOptionElement).value),
  );
  console.log("option count:", values.length);

  const chosen = values[Math.floor(Math.random() * values.length)];
  console.log("chose:", chosen);

  // Build the locator from the choice, then prove it before acting on it.
  const optionXPath = "//option[@value='" + chosen + "']";
  await expect(page.locator("xpath=" + optionXPath)).toHaveCount(1);

  await page.locator("#Skills").selectOption(chosen);
  await expect(page.locator("#Skills")).toHaveValue(chosen);
});
```

**The `toHaveCount(1)` step is the whole exercise.** A dynamic locator is a string you built at runtime, and a string you built can be wrong — a stray space, an apostrophe in the value, an option that was not there today. Asserting the count *before* you act turns a confusing "element not found, waited 30 s" into an immediate, readable "expected 1, got 0".

It also guards a nastier failure: if your expression accidentally matched **two** options, `selectOption` would quietly take one of them and the test would pass while testing something you did not intend.

**Reading the options rather than hard-coding them** is the other half. `#Skills` carries 78 of them; a list you typed out by hand is wrong the day someone edits the page.

> Run it several times. Different option each run, still passing — that is the property you want from dynamic data.

3:
Add the generator to `week2/generated/registration-helpers.ts`:

```ts
/** Digits only - the tel field rejects anything else. */
export function uniquePhone(): string {
  return "555" + Math.floor(Math.random() * 10_000_000).toString().padStart(7, "0");
}
```

Then a spec that fills the form twice and asserts both values:

```ts
import { test, expect } from "@playwright/test";
import { fillRegistrationForm, uniquePhone } from "./registration-helpers";

test("two runs of the same form get two different phone numbers", async ({ page }) => {
  const phoneOne = uniquePhone();
  await fillRegistrationForm(page, "learner.one@example.com");
  await page.locator("input[type='tel']").fill(phoneOne);
  await expect(page.locator("input[type='tel']")).toHaveValue(phoneOne);

  const phoneTwo = uniquePhone();
  await fillRegistrationForm(page, "learner.two@example.com"); // re-navigates, clearing the form
  await page.locator("input[type='tel']").fill(phoneTwo);
  await expect(page.locator("input[type='tel']")).toHaveValue(phoneTwo);

  expect(phoneOne).not.toBe(phoneTwo);
});
```

**`padStart` is doing real work.** Without it, `Math.floor(Math.random() * 10_000_000)` occasionally produces a short number, and you get a six-digit phone where the test expected ten. A generator that is *usually* the right shape is the kind of flakiness that appears once a fortnight and is miserable to chase.

**Why no `@example.com` suffix.** The email generator's suffix makes a valid address; a `tel` field rejects it. Each generator's rule comes from the field it feeds, which is why this is a separate function rather than a parameter on the email one.

**The final `expect(phoneOne).not.toBe(phoneTwo)`** asserts the generator's actual contract. Without it the test still passes if `uniquePhone()` returns a constant — and a "unique" generator that is not unique is exactly the bug worth catching.
